//? Title: Factor Analysis: Common Variance, Rotation and Boundary Fits
//? Subtitle: Exact three-indicator calculations, principal-axis diagnostics and Iris
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Several measured variables can move together because they share fewer unobserved
sources of variation. We want to represent their covariance by common loadings and
variable-specific variance, while checking whether that split is statistically and
numerically plausible. An exact three-indicator example exposes the algebra and
principal-axis iterations; a change of measurement units and an orthogonal rotation
show what is invariant. Repeated synthetic samples check recovery of known common
covariances and compare a rank-matched PCA. Finally, Iris demonstrates why a stopped
iteration, a nonnegative parameter count and a small residual do not by themselves
establish an admissible or scientifically interpretable factor model. Its one-factor
fit stops with a negative raw uniqueness for petal length.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Model common covariance and separate unique variance

For a centred p-vector, k common factors and independent variable-specific errors,

$$
 x=\Lambda f+\epsilon,\qquad \operatorname{Cov}(f)=I_k,\quad
 \operatorname{Cov}(f,\epsilon)=0,\quad \operatorname{Cov}(\epsilon)=\Psi,
 \qquad \Sigma=\Lambda\Lambda^T+\Psi.
$$
\alttext(Observed variables are linear combinations of unit-variance uncorrelated factors plus unique errors. Their covariance is the loading Gram matrix plus a diagonal uniqueness matrix.)

Here Lambda is p by k and Psi is diagonal with nonnegative entries. No normality
assumption is needed for this covariance identity. The generated studies below use
Gaussian factors/errors, but the native estimator is principal-axis factoring (PAF),
not Gaussian maximum likelihood. R's `factanal` and scikit-learn's FactorAnalysis
therefore solve different estimation problems and need not return the same fit.

For one factor, off-diagonal covariance is lambda_i*lambda_j. A unique error contributes
only to its own diagonal. This restriction is what distinguishes the common-factor
model from an unrestricted low-rank approximation.

The retained TikZ diagram is rendered locally. Regeneration requires LaTeX with
PGF/TikZ, standalone and Latin Modern, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/factor_model.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/factor_model.tikz > build/factor_model.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/factor_model.svg build/factor_model.dvi > build/factor_model.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local common-factor diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local common-factor diagram.
// \image(figures/factor_model.svg)
// result_end
\caption(One common source creates covariance among three indicators; independent unique terms add only diagonal variance.)
\alttext(A common latent factor points to three observed variables, with one loading on each arrow. Three separate errors point to their respective observations. Equations show shared cross-covariance and the common-plus-unique variance split.)

>> Communalities, uniquenesses and improper fits

The common variance for feature j is the squared norm of its loading row:

$$
 h_j^2=\sum_{c=1}^k\lambda_{jc}^2,\qquad
 \psi_j=C_{jj}-h_j^2,\qquad
 \text{common fraction}_j=\frac{h_j^2}{C_{jj}}.
$$
\alttext(Communality is the sum of squared loadings. Raw uniqueness is observed variance minus communality, and their ratio measures the feature's common fraction.)

With standardized features, C is the sample correlation matrix and C_jj is one up to
roundoff. Without standardization, communalities and uniquenesses are variances in
each feature's units; dividing by C_jj produces a dimensionless fraction. A negative
raw uniqueness is an improper solution, often called an ultra-Heywood case; a zero
uniqueness is a boundary case. We use “Heywood violation” below for materially negative
raw uniqueness. Such a violation can occur even when the parameter count is favourable.

The existing `uniquenesses()` API clips negative raw values to zero for compatibility.
The new `variance_table()` shows both values, actual squared loading norms and a
Heywood flag. Consequently, `reproduced_covariance()` can exceed an observed diagonal:
clipping is not a repair of an improper loading solution.

>> Derive principal-axis updates and their stopping rule

Let C be the covariance of the centred, optionally standardized complete rows. Sample
standard deviations and C use the n−1 divisor. Initialize with squared multiple
correlations, then repeatedly replace the diagonal by estimated common variances:

$$
 h_{j,0}^2=C_{jj}\operatorname{clip}\!\left(1-
 \frac{1}{(C_{\mathrm{floor}}^{-1})_{jj}C_{jj}},0,1\right),\quad
 C_t=C-\operatorname{diag}(C)+\operatorname{diag}(h_t^2),\quad
 L_{t+1}=V_k\operatorname{diag}\!\left(\sqrt{\max(\lambda_1,0)},\ldots,
 \sqrt{\max(\lambda_k,0)}\right).
$$
\alttext(Initial common variances are observed variances times squared multiple correlations. Each reduced matrix substitutes those variances on the diagonal, then its leading nonnegative eigenvalues and eigenvectors produce updated loadings.)

The next diagonal is clip(row_squared_norm(L),0,C_jj). The native implementation stops
when the largest absolute change in these **clipped** variances is less than `tol`, or
when `max_iter` is exhausted. The published loadings remain the actual eigenvectors
times square roots, so their squared row norms need not equal the clipped diagonal.
`iteration_history()` records every completed update and its residual/violation count;
`converged()` reports only this extraction stopping condition, not validity or a global
minimum. PAF is neither maximum likelihood nor a guaranteed minimum-residual optimizer.

The inverse used for SMCs and scores floors eigenvalues at 1e-8 times the largest
observed feature variance. This is a regularized inverse, not a Moore–Penrose inverse;
near singularity, scores and initialization depend on that convention. Symmetric
matrices are normalized before the Jacobi eigensolve. Forming covariance still loses
relative accuracy in poorly conditioned directions. Each PAF step costs O(p cubed)
for bounded Jacobi sweeps, after O(n*p squared) preprocessing; stored history adds
O(iterations*p) space. Only finite complete rows with positive feature variances are
accepted. Missing-feature rows are dropped together, and their original indices are
retained for score/label alignment. Feature names must be numeric and distinct.

>> Rotation changes the coordinates, not the common covariance

For orthogonal Q,

$$
 (\Lambda Q)(\Lambda Q)^T=\Lambda\Lambda^T,\qquad
 V(Q)=\sum_c\left[\sum_j q_{jc}^4-
 \frac{1}{p}\left(\sum_jq_{jc}^2\right)^2\right],\qquad
 q_{jc}=\frac{(\Lambda Q)_{jc}}{\sqrt{h_j^2}}.
$$
\alttext(Orthogonal rotation preserves the common covariance. Kaiser-normalized varimax increases the sum of within-column variances of squared row-normalized loadings; zero loading rows contribute zero.)

The implementation uses pairwise varimax sweeps, then orders columns by squared
loading sums and makes each largest-magnitude loading positive. These are reporting
conventions, not identification of physical causes. The rotation objective is computed
on row-normalized loadings and the row norms are restored afterwards. A higher value
can give a simpler loading pattern without improving covariance fit. Varimax retains
orthogonal factors; correlated generating factors require oblique coordinates to retain
their original simple pattern. The common covariance is a safer recovery target than
individual loading entries when signs, permutations or rotations can change.

>> Scores and parameter counting answer different questions

For preprocessed rows Z, regression scores use

$$
 \widehat F=ZC_{\mathrm{floor}}^{-1}\Lambda,\qquad
 \mathrm{df}=\frac{p(p+1)}2-
 \left(pk+p-\frac{k(k-1)}2\right)
 =\frac{(p-k)^2-p-k}{2}.
$$
\alttext(Regression scores use the observed regularized covariance inverse. Parameter-count degrees of freedom subtract loadings and unique variances, accounting for orthogonal rotation freedom, from observed covariance entries.)

The scores are linear estimates of unobserved factors. They are generally shrunken,
not unit variance, and not guaranteed sample-orthogonal. Orthogonal rotation of a fit
rotates its scores too. Nonnegative df is a necessary parameter-count check for generic
identification, not a sufficient uniqueness or admissibility test. Negative df means the
unrestricted model has more effective parameters than available covariance entries;
the API allows such fits for inspection. These df are not a computed chi-square test,
and this PAF report supplies no likelihood-based p-value.

> Simple examples

>> Three indicators whose correlations are exactly one half

Take eight centred rows, with a=sqrt(7)/2. The three columns below have four nonzero
entries of magnitude a, so each sample variance is 4*a squared/7=1. Each pair has two
matching nonzero entries, giving covariance 2*a squared/7=.5. For a one-factor model,
lambda_1 squared=C_12*C_13/C_23=.5, and similarly for the other two indicators.
The positive-sign loadings are therefore sqrt(.5), and each uniqueness is .5.

The inverse correlation diagonal is 1.5, so every initial communality is 1/3. Symmetry
reduces the PAF update to a scalar recurrence:

$$
 h_{t+1}^2=\frac{h_t^2+1}{3},\qquad h_0^2=\frac13,\qquad
 h_t^2=\frac12-\frac{1}{6\cdot3^t}.
$$
\alttext(The equal communalities update by adding one and dividing by three. Starting at one third, the error from one half shrinks by a factor of three per iteration.)

The first update is 4/9. The table exposes that intermediate state rather than just
the final answer. For this example the regression weights are sqrt(.5)/2 on each
indicator, so a row's score is sqrt(.5)*(a+b+c)/2. Its sample score variance is .75,
even though the latent factor's model variance is one.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fa_exact.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const double           a = std::sqrt(7.) / 2;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("a", std::vector<double>{a, a, 0, 0, 0, 0, -a, -a});
  d.add_column("b", std::vector<double>{a, 0, a, 0, 0, -a, 0, -a});
  d.add_column("c", std::vector<double>{a, 0, 0, a, -a, 0, 0, -a});
  const dm::stats::FactorAnalysis m(d, {"a", "b", "c"}, {1, true, "none", 1000, 1e-12});
  const auto&                     h = m.iteration_history();
  auto split = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), path = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  split.bars({1, 2, 3}, {1, 1, 1}, "observed", {148, 163, 184}, .55)
      .bars({1, 2, 3}, m.communalities(), "common", {37, 99, 235}, .55)
      .title("Common and remaining variance")
      .x_label("indicator (a, b, c)")
      .y_label("sample variance")
      .y_limits(0, 1.5);
  path.line(
          h.numeric_column("iteration").values(),
          h.numeric_column("communality_1").values(),
          "updated h squared",
          {37, 99, 235})
      .line({1, double(m.iterations())}, {.5, .5}, "exact .5", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Each update divides the error by three")
      .x_label("PAF iteration")
      .y_label("communality of indicator a")
      .y_limits(.3, .7);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(split).add(path).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/fa_exact.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",
      d.to_md(5),
      m.loading_table().to_md(8),
      m.variance_table().to_md(8),
      h.select({"iteration", "communality_1", "max_change"}).head(5).to_md(9));
}
```
