\image(figures/fa_iris.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Iris separates a parameter-count check, iteration stopping, admissibility and covariance residuals.)
\alttext(Grouped bars show signed raw uniqueness for each Iris measurement under one and two factors, including negative values. Another panel compares six observed correlations with their reproduced values. Tables give sample rows, fit status, degrees of freedom, Heywood counts, one-factor variance splits and loadings.)

The one-factor Iris iteration stops after 22 steps, yet petal length has communality
1.0324913 and raw uniqueness −.0324913. Its reproduced diagonal exceeds one by exactly
that violation. The two-factor run reaches 1000 steps without convergence; despite
its smaller off-diagonal RMS (.0054321 versus .0780916), it has negative parameter-count
df and still has a negative raw uniqueness. These outcomes do not identify a single
cause for the misfit and do not support treating the one-factor fit as a clean solution.

> Conclusion

The exact three-indicator example connects the covariance model to loadings,
unique variances, a scalar PAF recurrence and shrunken regression scores. A uniform
change of units preserves common fractions, while orthogonal rotation preserves
common covariance rather than individual loading entries. Constructed samples make
rotation-invariant recovery checks possible and distinguish sample covariance fit
from PCA's total-variance reconstruction objective.

Iris illustrates why no single diagnostic certifies a factor model. Numerical
stopping, parameter counting, nonnegative unique variance and residual fit are
separate checks. Clamping a negative uniqueness does not make its loading solution
admissible. This implementation supplies exploratory PAF with orthogonal varimax;
it does not establish causal factors, perform oblique rotation, compute ML fit tests,
or quantify sampling uncertainty from one fitted dataset.

> References

- Revelle, W. [psych::fa: exploratory factor analysis|https://personality-project.org/r/psych/help/fa.html]. Principal-axis iteration, SMC initialization and regression-score conventions; this implementation uses maximum clipped-communality change, not a sum-change stopping rule.
- Kaiser, H. F. (1958). [The varimax criterion for analytic rotation in factor analysis|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289233]. Psychometrika 23, 187–200. Kaiser normalization and orthogonal rotation.
- R Core Team. [factanal|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/factanal.html]. Covariance model, uniqueness boundaries and regression scores; factanal fits maximum likelihood rather than the PAF estimator used here.
- Thomson, G. H. (1938). [Methods of Estimating Mental Factors|https://doi.org/10.1038/141246a0]. Nature 141, 246. Factor-score estimation.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. Annals of Eugenics 7, 179–188. Iris dataset context.

